#!/usr/bin/env node

// Stamps a per-feature tippecanoe `minzoom` on soundings so a dense survey
// thins with zoom SHOALEST FIRST.
//
// Usage:
//   bin/thin-soundings --minzoom N --maxzoom N <SOUNDG.geojson> ...
//
// Each input file is rewritten in place. Only POINT features are touched; the
// pipeline exports with SPLIT_MULTIPOINT=ON, so every sounding is its own point
// feature, and a MultiPoint that reaches this is left alone rather than thinned
// as a lump.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS
//
// Until the zoom partition, SOUNDG was the ONE class tippecanoe's dot-dropping
// was right for: -r/--drop-rate throws away a fraction of the points at every
// zoom below the maxzoom, chosen by a hash of position, and which soundings go
// was not load-bearing because the contours and depth areas carry the same
// information at that scale.
//
// The partition removes that. Every feature of a partitioned cell carries a
// per-feature tippecanoe `minzoom`, and a per-feature minzoom is tippecanoe's
// documented exemption from dot-dropping -- the feature is "preserved down to
// that zoom level even if dot-dropping with -r would otherwise have dropped
// it". So the moment soundings transition with their cell, nothing thins them
// at all, and the overview tiles of a modern survey carry every sounding in it.
//
// The decision is therefore made here, by depth, before tippecanoe sees the
// features -- and made better than the drop rate made it, because a hash of
// position is depth-blind: it is as likely to keep the 40 m sounding as the
// 2.1 m one beside it, and a chart that shows the deep one alone says the water
// is clear where the shoal is.
//
// THE RULE
//
// At each zoom the world is cut into cells of about 64 screen pixels. In each
// cell, the SHOALEST sounding wins that zoom, and a feature's stamped `minzoom`
// is the shallowest zoom it ever wins at. A sounding that never wins is stamped
// with the band maxzoom, where the grid is finest and the survey is drawn in
// full.
//
// The rule is monotone, which is what makes the result stable: a cell at z+1 is
// a quarter of a cell at z, so a sounding that was shoalest in the larger cell
// is still shoalest in the smaller one. A winner never stops winning, and the
// zoom it first wins at is therefore also every zoom after it -- the survivor
// set at a zoom is a superset of the one below it, which is what makes the
// lattice look like a survey thinning rather than a set of unrelated pickings.
// Losers stay in the competition at every zoom for the same reason as in
// bin/generate-hazard-minzooms: the winner from a coarser zoom is still on the
// chart, and skipping it would let a deeper sounding claim the cell it already
// occupies and double the density there.
//
// This is bin/generate-hazard-minzooms' rule over a different population and a
// different ranking attribute, and deliberately so -- the two layers thin
// against a screen grid of the same size, so a hazard symbol and a sounding
// figure do not collide any worse than two soundings do. What it is NOT is one
// competition: a hazard and a sounding in the same square are two different
// statements about that square and both belong on the chart.
//
// WHAT THE RULE DOES NOT BOUND
//
// The competition is per S-57 CELL, because that is the unit this runs on: one
// invocation of bin/s57-to-tiles converts one .000 and passes it that cell's
// SOUNDG export. Neighbouring cells are separate runs that never see each
// other's soundings, so "one sounding per 64 px" is true of a chart cell and
// not of the screen. See the same paragraph in bin/generate-hazard-minzooms;
// the honest place for a cross-cell rule is after tile-join.
//
// THE ZOOM PARTITION
//
// A partitioned cell arrives as zoom-range COPIES of each sounding -- one per
// interval of the copy ladder, carrying _QZMIN/_QZMAX (and _QFALL on a fallback
// continuation) -- which bin/stamp-quilt-zooms turns into tippecanoe bounds
// after this runs, composing minzoom by max() and maxzoom by min().
//
// So a copy competes ONLY at the zooms its own interval serves, and two things
// turn on that.
//
//   * Correctness of the thinning. A copy that does not serve a zoom must not
//     hold a grid cell at it, or it evicts the copy that does.
//   * Determinism. The copies of one sounding share a position, a DEPTH and an
//     LNAM, so nothing in the ranking can tell them apart. Restricted to their
//     own intervals -- which are disjoint by construction -- at most one of
//     them is ever in the same contest.
//
// What is written is the THINNING minzoom alone. Composing it with _QZMIN here
// would stamp the partition floor twice and strip nothing; the properties stay
// exactly as they arrived, because composing them is bin/stamp-quilt-zooms'
// job and it needs them to do it. A copy that wins nothing inside its own
// interval is stamped with the band maxzoom, which composes to an empty range
// and publishes nothing THERE -- which is the thinning decision, said in the
// only vocabulary the tippecanoe member has: another copy of that sounding
// serves the zooms above it.
//
// EFFECTIVE DEPTH
//
// DEPTH, in metres, from the S-57 export's ADD_SOUNDG_DEPTH=ON, and shallower
// wins -- so a drying height (negative DEPTH) outranks everything wet. A
// sounding with NO readable DEPTH sorts as the DEEPEST there is, which is the
// opposite of the hazard rule and for the opposite reason: a wreck with no
// VALSOU is a hazard of unknown least depth, which S-52 treats as the dangerous
// case, while a sounding with no depth is not a shoal claim at all and cannot
// displace one.
//
// Ties break on LNAM and then on position, so the same chart always produces
// the same tiles. LNAM alone is not enough here and is in
// bin/generate-hazard-minzooms: SPLIT_MULTIPOINT=ON splits one S-57 sounding
// record into a point feature per depth and every one of them carries the
// record's LNAM, so a tie on LNAM is the common case rather than the rare one,
// and breaking it on export order would make the output depend on how ogr2ogr
// happened to walk the record.

import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from "fs";

// Screen size of a thinning cell, in pixels. A sounding figure is ~10 px of
// ink; 64 px is bin/generate-hazard-minzooms' spacing, so the two layers thin
// against the same grid.
const CELL_PIXELS = 64;

// The tile grid the pixel size is measured against. Matches bin/s57-to-tiles.
const TILE_PIXELS = 512;

const args = process.argv.slice(2);
let minzoom = 0;
let maxzoom = 16;
const inputPaths = [];

for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
  if (args[i] === "--minzoom" && args[i + 1]) {
    minzoom = parseInt(args[++i], 10);
  } else if (args[i] === "--maxzoom" && args[i + 1]) {
    maxzoom = parseInt(args[++i], 10);
  } else {
    inputPaths.push(args[i]);
  }
}

if (inputPaths.length === 0) {
  console.error(
    "Usage: thin-soundings --minzoom N --maxzoom N <SOUNDG.geojson> ...",
  );
  process.exit(1);
}

/** Web Mercator, normalized to [0, 1) on both axes. */
function mercator(lon, lat) {
  const x = (lon + 180) / 360;
  // Clamped short of the poles, where the projection diverges: an S-57 cell
  // never reaches them, and a NaN here would silently unbucket the feature.
  const clamped = Math.max(-85.05112878, Math.min(85.05112878, lat));
  const sin = Math.sin((clamped * Math.PI) / 180);
  const y = 0.5 - Math.log((1 + sin) / (1 - sin)) / (4 * Math.PI);
  return [x, y];
}

/**
 * Charted depth, shallower first. An unreadable one is the deepest there is.
 *
 * Returned as a number so the comparison is a plain `<`; the sentinel has to be
 * above every real sounding, and no charted depth is infinite.
 */
function chartedDepth(properties) {
  const value = Number(properties?.DEPTH);
  return Number.isFinite(value) ? value : Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
}

/**
 * The zoom interval a partitioned copy serves; see THE ZOOM PARTITION. An
 * absent or unreadable bound binds nothing, which is every legacy invocation.
 */
function zoomBound(value, fallback) {
  if (value === null || value === undefined || value === "") return fallback;
  const number = Number(value);
  return Number.isFinite(number) ? number : fallback;
}

const soundings = [];
const documents = [];

for (const path of inputPaths) {
  const document = JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, "utf8"));
  documents.push({ path, document });
  const features = document.features ?? [];
  for (const feature of features) {
    if (feature?.geometry?.type !== "Point") continue;
    const coordinates = feature.geometry.coordinates;
    if (!Array.isArray(coordinates) || coordinates.length < 2) continue;
    const [lon, lat] = coordinates;
    if (!Number.isFinite(lon) || !Number.isFinite(lat)) continue;
    const properties = feature.properties ?? {};
    soundings.push({
      feature,
      depth: chartedDepth(properties),
      // The identity a tie is broken on, and it must not depend on the order
      // the file happened to be written in: see the header on LNAM.
      key: `${properties.LNAM ?? ""}\u0000${lon},${lat}`,
      mercator: mercator(lon, lat),
      qzmin: zoomBound(properties._QZMIN, Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY),
      qzmax: zoomBound(properties._QZMAX, Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY),
      // Filled in below. Nothing that reaches this array leaves without one.
      minzoom: maxzoom,
    });
  }
}

/** True when `a` is the shoaler of the two, ties broken on identity. */
function outranks(a, b) {
  if (a.depth !== b.depth) return a.depth < b.depth;
  return a.key < b.key;
}

for (let zoom = minzoom; zoom < maxzoom; zoom++) {
  // Cells per axis at this zoom. 2^zoom tiles of TILE_PIXELS, cut into
  // CELL_PIXELS squares.
  const cells = Math.pow(2, zoom) * (TILE_PIXELS / CELL_PIXELS);
  const winners = new Map();
  for (const sounding of soundings) {
    if (zoom < sounding.qzmin || zoom > sounding.qzmax) continue;
    const [x, y] = sounding.mercator;
    const column = Math.min(cells - 1, Math.floor(x * cells));
    const row = Math.min(cells - 1, Math.floor(y * cells));
    const cell = `${column},${row}`;
    const held = winners.get(cell);
    if (held === undefined || outranks(sounding, held))
      winners.set(cell, sounding);
  }
  for (const winner of winners.values()) {
    // Ascending zooms, so the first zoom a feature wins at is the shallowest,
    // and monotonicity means it wins every zoom after it too.
    if (winner.minzoom === maxzoom) winner.minzoom = zoom;
  }
}

for (const sounding of soundings) {
  // NOT a property: tippecanoe reads this member off the Feature itself,
  // beside `geometry`. See bin/s57-to-tiles step 4.
  sounding.feature.tippecanoe = {
    ...(sounding.feature.tippecanoe ?? {}),
    minzoom: sounding.minzoom,
  };
}

for (const { path, document } of documents) {
  writeFileSync(path, JSON.stringify(document));
}

const early = soundings.filter((sounding) => sounding.minzoom < maxzoom).length;
console.error(
  `Thinned ${soundings.length} sounding(s) across z${minzoom}-${maxzoom}: ` +
    `${early} visible before z${maxzoom}`,
);
